--- D jidou/movement/under_member_placement (3) ---
== FINDINGS (every red above is one of these three; none is a
   weakened assertion, and none is a byte-splice artefact -- the
   a0 skew probe is GREEN at commit dc44ccf1, and every wire string
   this file compares against decoded exactly)

   F1 ENGINE BUG -- a count>1 mandatory SelectCard sourced from a
      NON-HAND zone accepts only ONE index.  PL!-pb2-017-P+ 登場
      raises zone="discard" count=4 (the engine's own trace:
      'Select 4 card(s) from the waiting room', nf=4) and a
      Rust-faithful answer of {0,1,2,3} places ONE card.
      rb_resume_with_choice_indices_internal (src/ability/choice.c)
      derives selected_idx = selected_indices[0] in the function's
      first lines, routes the multi-index arm through
      choice_handle_hand_indices (defined at choice.c:1311, called at
      :4255) which covers HAND zones only, and on decline falls back to
      rb_resolver_handle_select_card at :4268 with `selected` = selbuf,
      built from indices[0] ALONE at :4137.  The engine's own log then
      reads '[DISCARD_PICK_REJECTED] ... mapped=0'.  Rust dispatches the
      WHOLE answer (result_handlers.rs:41-65, indices.to_vec()).
      (choice.c is under concurrent peer edit, so the FUNCTION names
      are the durable anchor; the line numbers are as of the working
      tree at commit dc44ccf1 with a peer's uncommitted choice.c.)
      PAIRED POSITIVE that proves it non-vacuous: b1-b4 use the SAME
      prompt kind, the SAME route=3, the SAME move_cards action and
      the SAME resume function with count=1, and they place the card.
      Census is CONSTANT, so the three unplaced cards are left in the
      waitroom, not destroyed.

   F2 ENGINE BUG -- a 起動 with a sequential_cost pays NEITHER leg
      and its same_area placement DESTROYS the retrieved card.
      PL!HS-bp1-002-RM: active energy 9 -> 9 across the activation
      (the 2E leg did not run) and SAYAKA is still standing in the
      area she was played to (the move-self-to-waitroom leg did not
      run), yet the EFFECT runs and its selection prompt is answered.
      After the answer the fixture card is in NO zone: waitroom 1->0
      and the whole-board census 92->91.  b5 is the paired control --
      no prompt is offered (the card fails the group gate) and the
      census stays 92->92 -- so the loss is on the answer path.
      Rust's own file insists the cost must be paid for this very
      reason ('the cost is what vacates the area').

   F3 ENGINE GAP -- there is no OFFER for a staged member's 起動,
      so 「のみ起動できる」 is unmeasurable end to end.  See
      require_offer_instrument() for the line-level evidence.

    NOT PORTED, and deliberately so.  All three are already covered
    by peers, verified by body rather than by header:
      live_success arm  (discard_group_member_placed_under_self_on_live_success)
          -> test_parity_int_gaps.c  c1_kinako_live_success_tucks_liella_member
             (and its paired negative, the same board before 成功)
      area-move arm    (discard_group_member_placed_under_self_on_position_change)
          -> test_parity_jidou_move_yell.c:1566
             test_kinako_tucks_group_member_under_self_on_area_move
      both_players     (under_member_counted_for_both_players)
          -> test_parity_jidou_extra.c 851-897 section I
             test_under_member_tuck_works_for_both_players
    Porting them again would add no coverage.  The one thing the
    survey got WRONG in that cluster is recorded instead:
      RUST FIXTURE BUG: under_member_counted_for_both_players_test.rs
      comments PL!SP-pb1-014-N as 「嵐 千砂都 ... a genuinely different
      『Liella!』 member」 in contrast to the きな子 host, but the card
      it actually tucks under きな子 is played as PL!SP-pb2-025-N --
      which cards.json ALSO calls 嵐 千砂都 (cost 2 blade 1 vs cost 2
      blade 2).  So the tucked card and the member doing the tucking
      are the SAME CHARACTER, and the comment's 'genuinely different'
      is false.  test_parity_jidou_extra.c:106 repeats the same false
      comment.  This file does not assert on it.

